  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Thunderstorms this afternoon/evening with locally heavy rain,\n  minor runoff concerns near burn scars and susceptible terrain.\n\n- Steady precipitation tonight into Saturday south of I-90.\n\n- Warming and drying trend Sunday into the first part of next\n  week.\n\nThis afternoon through Saturday afternoon: Broad-scale lift and \npartial clearing north of an upper low over southwest Idaho will \ntrigger rounds of showers and thunderstorms this afternoon and \nevening, primarily from I-90 southward. Weak steering flow \ncombined with anomalous moisture (precipitable water 150-200% of \nnormal) will make locally heavy rainfall the primary hazard. \nTypical rain rates of 0.25-0.50 in/hr are expected, with localized\ncores exceeding 0.75 in/hr. This poses a localized risk for \nstanding water, rapid runoff, and debris flows over burn scars and\nsusceptible terrain.\n\nThe upper low will slide across southern Idaho tonight into \nSaturday, spreading a band of wrap-around stratiform rain with \nembedded convection from I-90 south into Lemhi County. \nPrecipitation will taper off late Saturday afternoon and evening. \nStorm-total rainfall will range from 0.50-1.50 inches from \nnorthern Lemhi County east to Butte, with isolated totals \nexceeding 2.0 inches.\n\nSunday through next weekend: Clearing skies Saturday night will \nfavor valley fog and low stratus, particularly in areas that \nreceived substantial rainfall. Upper-level ridging builds across \nthe region Sunday and Monday, establishing a warming and drying \ntrend.\n\nWhile deterministic models diverge on the details mid-to-late \nnext week, ensemble clusters favor southwesterly flow, mild \ntemperatures, increased afternoon breezes, and a gradual return of\nisolated afternoon thunderstorm chances starting as early as \nTuesday. By Sunday (Sep 27), ensemble guidance (60-70% confidence)\npoints toward a deeper Pacific Northwest trough pushing inland, \nbringing cooler temperatures and higher shower chances.",
